 { In the next old, but instructive example the stronger side will have more difficulties to find a shelter for his king, but he finally solves the issue.Material is equal but Black's advanced e-pawn yields him a clear advantage. } 1... { -KEY- } e4 $1 { Black should advance the passed pawn as far as possible, without bothering about the enemy queen checks, just like in the previous example. } 2. Qb5+ Kd4 $1 { We already know that in queen endings, the sheltering possibilities tend to be higher in enemy territory. } 3. Qb6+ Kd3 4. Qa6+ Ke3 $1 { The h-pawn doesn't matter, the main thing is to assist the e-pawn. } ( { After } 4... Kc2 $6 5. Qa4 $1 { , the king would have to return. } ) 
5. Qxh6+ Ke2 { Thanks to his clever first move, Black can calmly offer a queen exchange. } 6. Qh5+ Ke1 7. g4 $2 ( { White's checks are over for the moment, so he clears some space for his queen, and advances his own passed pawn. However, this natural move also has the defect of clearing the e1-h4 diagonal for Black's queen, which cannot have been easy to establish over the board, without moving the pieces! For this reason, passive defense with } 7. Qf5 { was called for. Farciarz provides the variation } e3 8. Qe4 e2 9. Kg2 Kd1 10. Qb1 Qc1 11. Qd3 Ke1 12. Kg1 Qc5 13. Kg2 Qf2 14. Kh1 { (this is the moment where Black, contrary to the game, is missing a queen check on h4) } Kf1 15. Qb1 e1=Q $11 16. Qd3 { with a perpetual despite the presence of two black queens! Well, I wouldn't blame Marshall for not seeing all these intricacies over the board. In practical play, White's task was difficult from the very beginning, as long as human players are involved, obviously. } ) 
e3 8. Qc5 ( { In the event of } 8. g5 e2 9. g6 { a good plan for Black would be to bring his king to a shelter square on a1; e.g. } Kf2 ( { not } 9. Qc5 Kd1 $2 10. g7 { and the d-pawn is pinned } ) 
10. Qh2 Kf3 11. Qh3 Ke4 12. Qg2 Kd4 13. Qg4 Kc3 14. Qg3 Kb2 15. Qe5 Kb1 16. Qe4 Ka1 17. Qe5 Qb2 { and wins. } ) 
Kd1 ( { Of course not } 8... e2 $4 9. Qg1 ) 
9. Qf5 e2 $1 { Whenever it can, the passed pawn should advance. } ( { In contrast, the game went } 9... Qf2 $2 10. Qb1 Ke2 11. Qb5 Kf3 12. Qd5 { and White later managed a perpetual, now that the black queen can no longer neutralize the side checks from b1 and b2. } ) 
10. Qb1+ Qc1 11. Qd3+ Ke1 12. Kg2 ( { Obviously, } 12. Qg3 { runs into } Kd2 { (with check). After the text only one step remains for Black to win the game: dislodge the enemy king from the g-file in order to shelter his own king on g1. } ) 
Qc6+ $1 13. Kg1 Qc5+ 14. Kg2 Qf2+ $1 15. Kh3 ( { Or } 15. Kh1 Qh4 16. Kg2 Qxg4 17. Kh2 Kf2 $1 { , catching White's king in a mating net. } ) 
Kf1 16. g5 Kg1 { Thanks to the shelter provided by the g5-pawn, Black's king can no longer be harassed by checks, and the e-pawn queens on the next move. } 
